39 0 obj
<</K 19/P 102 0 R/Pg 10 0 R/S/H4/T(Abstract: Although loneliness is a growing public health issue in the United States, its political effects remain understudied. Using data from the Chapman Survey of American Fears \(Wave 11\), this study evaluates whether loneliness predicts support for \
political violence. Results contradict expectations: loneliness does not increase support for political violence and, instead, reduces willingness to damage property for a political cause. Younger adults \(18…34\) show the strongest support for political \
violence regardless of loneliness level, while partisanship is not significant once demographic controls are included. These findings challenge assumptions that lonely people are more prone to extremism and suggest political violence attitudes stem from g\
enerational and cultural forces rather than psychosocial distress.)>>
endobj
40 0 obj
<</K 22/P 102 0 R/Pg 10 0 R/S/H3/T(Introduction:)>>
endobj
41 0 obj
<</K 21/P 102 0 R/Pg 10 0 R/S/H5/T(Loneliness is associated with depression, anxiety, weakened immunity, and early mortality, but its political consequences remain understudied. In recent years, the United States has experienced rising threats against political officials, growing polarizat\
ion, and increased acceptance of political violence. Because loneliness influences social cognition, emotional regulation, and threat processing, scholars theorize that it may shape political behavior. This project investigates whether loneliness predicts\
 support for political violence and whether age or partisanship condition this relationship.)>>
endobj
